      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mwbmsbt8-obb8",
      "title": "‘We said no, no, no, but they kept insisting’: the Paraguayan people battling a eucalyptus empire",
      "description": "<p>It is Paraguay’s largest private investment but local people accuse a forestry giant of ignoring their contamination concerns over its vast plantation</p><p>When Paracel arrived in Tacuarita, an Indigenous community of homes made from planks and plastic sheeting in Paraguay’s poorest region, six years ago, it was a welcome sight. The company improved the dirt roads and dug an electric-powered well, saving villagers a two-mile journey to fetch water from a cattle pond that often made them sick. It also gave dozens of local people forestry jobs.</p><p>“They fulfilled all their promises,” says Florencio Garcete, 55, chief of the Mbya Guaraní community of 211 people.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/nov/10/investigation-pulp-company-paraguay-paracel-eucalyptus-plantation\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/nov/10/investigation-pulp-company-paraguay-paracel-eucalyptus-plantation",
