      "easySummary": "<p>A 72-hour TV blizzard was a test of sofa stamina as Ireland and England shone while South Africa looked ominous</p><p>By Sunday night one question swamped all else. When does a diet of endless televised rugby become too much of a good thing? People are well used to three consecutive Six Nations games on “Super Saturday” but the weekend was on a whole new level. It must have ranked among the most relentless 72-hour blizzards of rugby broadcasting in the game’s history.</p><p>The Breakdown, naturally, was keen to get among it. Ireland v Argentina on ITV on Friday night had its moments, happily, although not as many as England A v Argentina on YouTube. On Saturday, with South Africa 45-0 up against Italy by half-time, there was a case for covering the screen with a Turin shroud. Either way it was time for the haka in Murrayfield before Scotland v New Zealand, subsequently interspersed with an absorbing Champ game between Cornish Pirates and Richmond on Clubber TV.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/10/rugby-union-breakdown-nations-championship-england-france-south-africa\">Continue reading...</a>",
