      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e3-2du2",
      "title": "The saga that never goes away: wounds of Australian cricket reopened as ball tampering resurfaces | Jack Snape",
      "description": "<p>With the issues raised in Cape Town still to be resolved, Usman Khawaja’s book threatens to damage reputations and divide cricket’s tight community</p><p>Australian cricket thought it had left its great shame behind, burying it in the days after Cameron Bancroft was shown on the big screen in Cape Town putting sandpaper down his pants.</p><p>Cricket Australia acted swiftly at the time, suspending the young batter, his captain Steve Smith and vice-captain David Warner. Too swiftly, according to Usman Khawaja, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/07/usman-khawaja-australia-cricket-team-sandpaper-ball-tampering-plot\">whose book released on Tuesday</a> criticised the investigation while Warner called it “rushed and botched”.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/10/sandpapergate-cricket-australia-ball-tampering-saga-usman-khawaja-book\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/10/sandpapergate-cricket-australia-ball-tampering-saga-usman-khawaja-book",
