aw enforcement agencies have been able to approve and award massive contracts for surveillance tech and rarely have any sort of trouble passing them,” said Reem Suleiman of the digital rights group Fight for the Future , which advocates against mass surveillance and hosts the site StopALPRs.com. “In 2026, this isn’t the case.” “People feel and understand that they do actually have a stake when it comes to surveillance tech and how it’s used.” Reem Suleiman, Fight for the Future The backlash has been building for some time, Suleiman said, pointing to the nonprofit Secure Justice’s tracker of nearly 500 actions by cities over the past year to pause or terminate Flock contracts. She also noted that the fight has moved beyond city councils — a Senate Judiciary hearing in late September showed bipartisan interest in reining in license plate readers, and federal and state proposals are multiplying. Because these decisions to employ ALPRs and other surveillance technologies have typically been made without public input, Suleiman said, putting the question to voters reflects a meaningful change: “People feel and understand that they do actually have a stake when it comes to surveillance tech and how it’s used.” Voting against local city use of Flock cameras, however, may be just the tip of the iceberg. Where the fight goes next The uptake of Flock cameras by nongovernment actors presents a separate accountability challenge that may complicate the efforts of anti-surveillance activists. Homeowners associations, hospitals, schools, and big retailers are contracting for the same technology, with no council meetings for citizens to pack and no ballots to vote on. According to reporting by Inc. , of the approximately 12,000 customers using Flock cameras, 5,000 are private-sector entities. These private buyers can opt to have the data generated by their cameras automatically shared with law enforcement. Many of them do just that, including commercial chains such as Home Depot and