      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e9-ged2",
      "title": "Racing must seize its chance as new commission tackles UK betting laws",
      "description": "<p>The 2005 Gambling Act has been described as ‘analogue’ in a digital age and review will be crucial for funding model</p><p>The prospect of a new Gambling Act, with significant implications for racing’s funding model via betting, moved a step closer on Monday. A new commission that includes experts from the fields of economics, law, public policy, health and sport will initiate an “independent, evidence-based assessment of the existing [legal] framework [around gambling] and consider whether further reform, including potential new primary legislation, may be required.”</p><p>The Gambling Policy Commission (GPC), which is chaired by Jagjit Chadha OBE, a professor of economics at Cambridge University, will launch on Monday with a “call for evidence from all interested parties and individuals”, to be submitted by 12 February 2027. It will then aim to deliver its findings and recommendations before the next general election.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/09/talking-horses-racing-uk-betting-laws\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/09/talking-horses-racing-uk-betting-laws",
