    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e9-do6f",
      "title": "Joe Burrow is reviving the Bengals and pulling away in the race for MVP",
      "description": "<p>The quarterback has led Cincinnati to four straight wins with stat lines that put him in the company of Tom Brady. At this rate, the trophy seems his to lose</p><p>At the end of the 2021 season, the Cincinnati Bengals came up three points short in Super Bowl LVI against the Los Angeles Rams. Near the end of the 2022 season, the Bengals came up three points short – a 23-20 score line again in the AFC championship game against the Kansas City Chiefs. But from 2023 through 2025, those Bengals had to walk the desert for a while. Between injuries to quarterback Joe Burrow, an offensive line that was problematic at best, and one of the NFL’s worst defenses from year to year, Cincinnati had a combined regular-season record of 24-27, and no playoff appearances.</p><p>Now, the new Bengals are 6-2 after their 34-17 shellacking of the Atlanta Falcons on Sunday at the Santiago Bernabéu stadium in Madrid. And Burrow is on a tear we’ve rarely seen from any quarterback. Over the last four weeks, his 16 touchdown passes is the most in a four-game span in Bengals history (which goes back to 1968). He is the second quarterback in pro football history with 16 or more passing touchdowns and two or more rushing touchdowns in any four-game span, putting him up with Tom Brady in 2007 – the season in which the New England Patriots’ offense scored 36.8 points per game, and Brady was at his best. Burrow has been pressured on just 30.2% of his dropbacks this season, as opposed to 33.8% in 2025 and 34.2% in 2024.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/09/joe-burrow-bengals-nfl-mvp-race-frontrunners\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/09/joe-burrow-bengals-nfl-mvp-race-frontrunners",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/09/joe-burrow-bengals-nfl-mvp-race-frontrunners",
