    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e8-4j78",
      "title": "Alan Jones obituary",
      "description": "<p>Australian driver who was the Formula One champion with the Williams team in 1980 and had 12 grand prix victories</p><p>“The championship? You take it any way it comes,” said Alan Jones, with typical unsentimental pithiness, after winning a tense Canadian Grand Prix in Montreal in 1980 that made him that year’s Formula One world champion. This came during his third season with the Williams Grand Prix team, after making a promising showing the year before with four race victories.</p><p>Jones, who has died aged 80 of cancer, had taken full advantage of the aerodynamic excellence of the new Williams FW07 car, featuring revolutionary “ground effect” technology, which was introduced in the latter part of the 1979 season.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/08/alan-jones-obituary\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/08/alan-jones-obituary",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/08/alan-jones-obituary",
