    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e8-7n91",
      "title": "Female sports journalists have always faced sexism. Social media has made it vicious and relentless | Anjali Doshi",
      "description": "<p>I have reported on cricket for two decades. The abuse I have been subjected to in recent years is shocking and demoralising</p><p>By <a href=\"https://www.wisden.com/wisden-cricket-monthly\">Wisden Cricket Monthly</a></p><p>Twenty-four years ago I walked into the Wankhede press box for the first time, to cover an India v England one-day international. That sultry Mumbai evening, Andrew Flintoff sprinted across the outfield twirling his shirt round his head as England won by five runs – a sight that so annoyed captain Sourav Ganguly that he would take his revenge on the Lord’s balcony later that summer.</p><p>I wrote my report for a local tabloid, the Afternoon Despatch &amp; Courier, with trepidation and an overwhelming sense of impostor syndrome. I was one of only two women among the contingent, and was terrified about what my male peers and senior colleagues would make of my presence and work.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/08/female-sports-journalists-sexism-social-media-vicious-relentless-cricket\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/08/female-sports-journalists-sexism-social-media-vicious-relentless-cricket",
