      "easySummary": "Scientists have uncovered evidence that Europe was dominated for more than 20 million years by open woodlands, colorful meadows, and large grazing animals, not the dense forests long considered its natural state. The surprising discovery challenges modern reforestation strategies, suggesting that planting thick forests could actually harm the biodiversity conservationists hope to protect.",
      "detailedSummary": "Published by ScienceDaily, this report highlights developments in Science & Technology.\n\nHeadline: \"Scientists discover Europe&#039;s ancient forests were nothing like we imagined\"\n\nScientists have uncovered evidence that Europe was dominated for more than 20 million years by open woodlands, colorful meadows, and large grazing animals, not the dense forests long considered its natural state. The surprising discovery challenges modern reforestation strategies, suggesting that planting thick forests could actually harm the biodiversity conservationists hope to protect.\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
