      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e6-b4nq",
      "title": "Sherrock’s synthetic success over Littler and why Netflix’s darts special felt so dissatisfying | Jonathan Liew",
      "description": "<p>Decades of professionalism, investment and industrialised training methods have rendered the gulf between the sporting gods and mortals virtually unbridgeable</p><p>“She walked in here a hairdresser from Milton Keynes – and walks out £501,000 richer!” Gary Lineker announced at the conclusion of Netflix’s Beat Luke Littler on Friday night. Which, while strictly accurate as a description of the winner Fallon Sherrock, arguably fell some way short of providing the full picture. “She walked in wearing socks – and walks out £501,000 richer!” “She walked in here a vertebrate mammal – and walks out £501,000 richer!” I mean, if we’re just reciting facts without context here.</p><p>Because while Sherrock may still theoretically be qualified to cut hair, much of her past decade has been spent on darts: playing darts, practising darts, travelling to and from darts, promoting her darts career. Though she may never have held a Professional Darts Corporation tour card, she is – by any sane definition of the term – a professional darts player. Indeed, as a result of her <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2019/dec/22/fallon-sherrock-first-woman-to-beat-man-pdc-world-darts-championship-billie-jean-king\">historic run to the third round of the world championship</a> at Alexandra Palace in late 2019, she may still be the second most famous darts player in the country, after Littler himself.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/05/darts-fallon-sherrock-synthetic-success-luke-littler-netflix\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/05/darts-fallon-sherrock-synthetic-success-luke-littler-netflix",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/05/darts-fallon-sherrock-synthetic-success-luke-littler-netflix",
