      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mwbmsbt9-2ag0",
      "title": "Kashmir’s potato pits are vanishing – and generations of knowledge could be buried for ever",
      "description": "<p>Farmers in Gurez store their harvests underground to last the Himalayan winter. As the practice fades, local varieties and handed-down skills are disappearing too</p><p>Hajra Begum rolls a potato in her hands, checking its skin for cuts, soft spots and signs of rot. After a day harvesting in her family’s field in Chorwan, a remote mountain village in Gurez, Indian-administered Kashmir, she is sorting the crop for winter storage. Some of the potatoes will feed her family through the cold months; others will be saved for planting in spring.</p><p>“It should be perfect for planting,” she says. “If you keep a bad potato, you will waste the whole next year.”</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/04/kashmir-potato-pits-vanishing-indigenous-knowledge-buried-aoe\">Continue reading...</a>",
