    {
      "id": "art-guardian_world-mwbmsbn7-hcic",
      "title": "The private creative world of Rabindranath Tagore, the man Yeats called ‘greater than any of us’",
      "description": "<p>A previously unseen family archive offers a glimpse into the Indian Nobel prize winner’s artistic<strong> </strong>process and personal relationships in his<strong> </strong>final years</p><p><strong>• </strong><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/30/sign-up-for-this-is-india-our-free-weekly-newsletter-on-life-in-the-subcontinent\"><strong>Don’t get This is India delivered to your inbox? Sign up here</strong></a></p><p>It’s easy to run out of words to describe Rabindranath Tagore. The first non-European to win the Nobel prize in literature, Tagore was a Bengali poet, musician, novelist, philosopher, songwriter, painter, humanist and educator.</p><p>Born in 1861, Tagore left an indelible mark on the subcontinent’s literature, music, education and art. Among his enduring legacies are the national anthems of India and Bangladesh.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/03/rabindranath-tagore-yeats-nobel-prize-india\">Continue reading...</a>",
