    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mwbms7e8-21cm",
      "title": "Champagne, cocaine and some very long walks: when pedestrianism ruled the sporting world",
      "description": "<p>Crowds once flocked to see ambling athletes pull off amazing feats of stamina. Edward Payson Weston was chief among them</p><p>More than $1.4bn has been <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/nov/02/prediction-markets-voters-elections\">staked on prediction markets</a> for this week’s US midterm elections, <a href=\"https://edition.cnn.com/2026/10/23/politics/prediction-markets-midterm-elections-kalshi-polymarket\">according to CNN</a>.</p><p>Though a lot of people are about to win – or lose – a lot of money, it’s hard to imagine any wagers as consequential as the election bet that birthed a sporting craze spearheaded by a chancer who walked and talked his way into becoming one of the greatest athletic stars of the 19th century.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/03/pedestrianism-long-distance-walking-edward-payson-weston\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/03/pedestrianism-long-distance-walking-edward-payson-weston",
