      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvxd1lhl-ev1w",
      "title": "Manchester United’s capitulation at Chelsea paints picture of a club that barely exists | Barney Ronay",
      "description": "<p>Michael Carrick is now a little more pre-doomed, pre-sacked, but is miles away from being the actual story</p><p>On and on and on. And then on just a little bit more it would seem. Manchester United: always crashing in the same Carrick.</p><p>The obvious question after <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/31/chelsea-manchester-united-premier-league-match-report\">this 2-0 defeat to Chelsea </a>is how much longer United will continue to lose games under the current manager, as opposed to losing games under the next one. But it is also a mark of the dysfunction of this club, its ambient levels of sadness, that this matters so little.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/31/manchester-united-chelsea-premier-league-michael-carrick\">Continue reading...</a>",
