      "quickSummary": [
        "Official verified dispatch from The Guardian Sport: \"Pakistan win tri-series final as Fakhar Zaman century denies Sri Lanka in thrilling chase\".",
        "Key Context: <ul><li><p>Opener scores 127 from 99 balls in Lahore</p></li><li><p>Kamindu Mendis’s 131 proves in vain for Sri Lanka</p></li></ul><p>This mini-tournament has been a welcome change of pace, carrying m.",
        "Categorized under Sports for global monitoring and reference.",
        "Original reporting published by The Guardian Sport. Direct canonical dispatch linked."
      ],
      "easySummary": "<ul><li><p>Opener scores 127 from 99 balls in Lahore</p></li><li><p>Kamindu Mendis’s 131 proves in vain for Sri Lanka</p></li></ul><p>This mini-tournament has been a welcome change of pace, carrying more meaning than your bog-standard three-match white-ball slog between two teams. And then there’s the gift of a final. This was a fitting conclusion under the lights in a packed-out Gaddafi Stadium in Lahore, settled in the penultimate over as Pakistan beat Sri Lanka by three wickets to win the tri-series.</p><p>Fakhar Zaman, Pakistan’s hero from the 2017 Champions Trophy final, crashed his way to 127 in a chase of 326. But plenty of work remained after his dismissal, and the new boys needed to stay calm. Arafat Minhas, 21, and Hasan Nawaz, 24, played like old-timers, their partnership of 94 securing the game. Minhas and Faheem Ashraf fell with two runs still required, but Shaheen Afridi finished the evening with a wallop over the ropes.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/31/pakistan-sri-lanka-tri-series-final\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian Sport, this report highlights developments in Sports.\n\nHeadline: \"Pakistan win tri-series final as Fakhar Zaman century denies Sri Lanka in thrilling chase\"\n\n<ul><li><p>Opener scores 127 from 99 balls in Lahore</p></li><li><p>Kamindu Mendis’s 131 proves in vain for Sri Lanka</p></li></ul><p>This mini-tournament has been a welcome change of pace, carrying more meaning than your bog-standard three-match white-ball slog between two teams. And then there’s the gift of a final. This was a fitting conclusion under the lights in a packed-out Gaddafi Stadium in Lahore, settled in the penultimate over as Pakistan beat Sri Lanka by three wickets to win the tri-series.</p><p>Fakhar Zaman, Pakistan’s hero from the 2017 Champions Trophy final, crashed his way to 127 in a chase of 326. But plenty of work remained after his dismissal, and the new boys needed to stay calm. Arafat Minhas, 21, and Hasan Nawaz, 24, played like old-timers, their partnership of 94 securing the game. Minhas and Faheem Ashraf fell with two runs still required, but Shaheen Afridi finished the evening with a wallop over the ropes.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/31/pakistan-sri-lanka-tri-series-final\">Continue reading...</a>\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
