    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvxd1pha-l6l1",
      "title": "‘Plantation tourism’: court victory signals hope in Jamaica’s fight to keep beaches public",
      "description": "<p>A temporary injunction in the coastline battle for beaches to be publicly accessible, not sealed off for private developments, is just a step in fighting what islanders call a tourism model that disadvantages Jamaicans</p><p>When the Australian human rights lawyer Eva Buzo emerged from the sea on to the shores of One Man Beach in Montego Bay on Sunday after her gruelling, nearly 11-hour-long swim, the thunderous pulse of welcome drums was drowned out by the chants of “free beach” from jubilant supporters.</p><p>Here in Jamaica’s tourism capital, against a backdrop of jagged mountains and sunset-tinted clouds, Buzo – a celebrated marathon swimmer – said she was pleased to help support the important work of the Jamaica Beach Birthright Environmental Movement (Jabbem) and others fighting for free public access to Jamaica’s beaches.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/31/jamaicans-beach-access-fight-court-victory-hope\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/31/jamaicans-beach-access-fight-court-victory-hope",
