      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian Sport, this report highlights developments in Sports.\n\nHeadline: \"How can playing for Manchester City fit into Marc Guéhi’s moral universe? | Barney Ronay\"\n\n<p>Club’s predicament raises interesting questions about principles, both for individuals and the Premier League</p><p><em>I felt unbelievably ponderous. My head was made of Aztec stone. I realised I didn’t want to be with all these people. Somebody came at me, a huge individual in silver and blue. I fell at his feet and grabbed one shoe. I started untying the lace. He kicked away from me. I got up and walked off. I was exceedingly hungry.</em></p><p>Not my words. Also not the words of the Manchester City centre-back Marc Guéhi, despite what you were thinking there. This is instead a condensed quote from the mind of Gary Harkness, existentially curious college running-back and the narrator of Don DeLillo’s great American football novel End Zone.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/30/manchester-city-financial-charges-guilty-marc-guehi-premier-league\">Continue reading...</a>\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
