    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvxd1lhu-n1kh",
      "title": "Exeter’s Tom Hooper: ‘Sometimes you’ve just got to suck up a little bit of pain’",
      "description": "<p>The Chiefs’ latest Wallaby on dealing with English weather, the positive side of rehab and Australia’s World Cup chances</p><p>The list of Australian rugby players who have found a spiritual home in Exeter is a long and illustrious one. Dean Mumm, Dave Dennis, Nic White, Scott Sio, Greg Holmes, Julian Salvi, Ben White, Mitch Lees, Lachie Turner, Len Ikitau … almost without exception they have pitched up in the south-west of England and relished the experience. As Rob Baxter, Exeter’s director of rugby, drily observes: “They seem to like the pasties and the cider.”</p><p>Perhaps the surfing opportunities and the area’s beguiling beaches have also played their part, although Baxter has his own subtly different theory: “Maybe Exeter and Devon is more like Australia than anywhere else in England. There’s plenty of open space and we tend to be a relatively old-school type of club. The Australian guys really seem to like that environment.”</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/30/exeters-tom-hooper-sometimes-youve-just-got-to-suck-up-a-little-bit-of-pain\">Continue reading...</a>",
