    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvxd1lhw-rfip",
      "title": "The low-budget Brewers won the World Series – and exposed baseball’s biggest lie | Howard Bryant",
      "description": "<p>Milwaukee’s <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/28/milwaukee-brewers-tampa-bay-rays-world-series-game-5-first-title\">first World Series championship</a> is a rebuke to baseball’s oligarch class, whose insistence that small-market teams cannot compete has been laid bare as fiction</p><p>For the entirety of the 2026 baseball season, the overlords of the game have stood on the single position that <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/28/milwaukee-brewers-tampa-bay-rays-world-series-game-5-first-title\">what has now happened</a> could not happen, and the result was a year that has been treated like an appetizer for the main course of the offseason: the shutting down of the game until the players, after 40 years of resistance, finally agree to a salary cap. The insistence that the game had been predetermined undermined the Milwaukee Brewers, who spent 149 days this season atop the standings.</p><p>Baseball dismissed <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/aug/18/brewers-mariners-22-0-modern-era-shutout-record\">Milwaukee’s 22-0 demolition of Seattle</a> on 18 August, the 20-0 waxing of Cincinnati a month later, and the comprehensive dusting of their division – their fourth consecutive National League Central title and fifth in six years – by 14 games. Baseball’s owners, at their persuasive best, would have their paying customers believe that the Brewers’ franchise-record 103 regular-season wins also were an illusion, or worse, the dreaded outlier, even though Milwaukee have won 770 games since 2018, the fourth-most in all of baseball. Even when Milwaukee exhaled and <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/16/los-angeles-dodgers-milwaukee-brewers-nlcs-payroll-world-series\">made the Dodgers look old</a> in a stirring four-game sweep, ESPN’s Jeff Passan reported the owners’ position remained unchanged.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/29/milwaukee-brewers-world-series-title-mlb-salary-cap\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/29/milwaukee-brewers-world-series-title-mlb-salary-cap",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/29/milwaukee-brewers-world-series-title-mlb-salary-cap",
      "publisherId": "the_guardian_sport",
      "creatorId": "the_guardian_sport",
      "creatorName": "The Guardian Sport",
      "creatorHandle": "@guardiansport",
      "creatorAvatar": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461896836934-ffe607ba8211?w=120&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80",
