      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvxd1phb-gfw5",
      "title": "Mark Carney is celebrated abroad – while consolidating power at home | Shiri Pasternak",
      "description": "<p>The Canadian prime minister’s reckoning with Trumpism in Davos clashes with his attacks on environmental, Indigenous and labour rights</p><p>The Canadian prime minister is a hero around the world <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/20/world/canada/carney-speech-davos-trump.html\">for his Davos speech</a> that reckoned with a reconfiguration of power in the shadow of Donald Trump.</p><p>But at home, Mark Carney is mirroring the US president by radically centralizing power within his office and cabinet and taking a hatchet to dozens of laws protecting <a href=\"https://thenarwhal.ca/carney-budget-environment-cuts/\">environmental</a>, <a href=\"https://amnesty.ca/human-rights-news/bill-c-5-sidelines-indigenous-rights/\">Indigenous</a> and <a href=\"https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/carney-bill-c-39-major-projects-labour-code-strikes-9.7354381\">labour rights</a>, all in order to enact his economic agenda.</p><p>Shiri Pasternak is an associate professor in criminology at Toronto Metropolitan University and a member of the Infrastructure Beyond Extractivism research team, a collaboration between critical scholars and Indigenous land defenders across North America</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/oct/28/mark-carney-trump-davos\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/oct/28/mark-carney-trump-davos",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/oct/28/mark-carney-trump-davos",
      "publisherId": "guardian_environment",
      "creatorId": "guardian_environment",
      "creatorName": "The Guardian Environment",
      "creatorHandle": "@guardianeco",
