    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvxd1lhy-f0pr",
      "title": "Marco Jansen belongs to a great tradition but top all-rounders are hard to find",
      "description": "<p>South African stands out, not just because of his height, but England and Australia now find themselves looking for an equivalent</p><p>If you were to bioengineer a cricketer in a laboratory, which attributes would you give them? You would make them tall, with long limbs and a whiplash action to extract steep bounce. Add speed, movement both ways and a left arm to offer a point of difference. With the bat, make sure they can whack sixes but also go down the gears when an innings starts to unravel. For an added trait, have them catch meteorites in the outfield and the cordon.</p><p>You might end up with Marco Jansen. At the start of the third Test, the 6ft 8in South African had taken 99 Test wickets at 20.81. His 85 against Australia at Gqeberha in the second Test came after South Africa had slumped to 110 for six before his counterattack helped turn a precarious position into an innings victory. Jansen is still developing as a batter, but his 25.66 average before this current Test understates his potential.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/28/cricket-marco-jansen-tradition-all-rounders-hard-to-find\">Continue reading...</a>",
