    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvxd1lhw-cev0",
      "title": "Kylian Mbappé’s China crisis shows how sensitive Mao’s legacy remains | Rebecca Bailey",
      "description": "<p>Real Madrid forward was dropped by brands for comments Chinese media generally steered clear of outlining </p><p>It’s been a bad start to the week for the Kylian Mbappé. The France forward missed out on joining the elite roster of <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/26/harry-kane-ends-englands-25-year-wait-for-a-mens-ballon-dor\">Ballon d’Or</a> winners and instead became the latest high-profile addition to a far less illustrious list – that of foreign brands and individuals charged with “hurting the feelings of the Chinese people”.</p><p>The saga began when a video of the Real Madrid player making light of a meme involving him and China’s former leader Mao Zedong began spreading.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/28/kylian-mbappe-china-crisis-shows-how-sensitive-maos-legacy-remains\">Continue reading...</a>",
