      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian Sport, this report highlights developments in Sports.\n\nHeadline: \"Jay-Z is the perfect Super Bowl shill for a league of billionaires\"\n\n<p>The rapper lends the NFL a veneer of credibility but is unlikely to push back on the establishment figures who run the league</p><p>On Monday the NFL <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/oct/27/jay-z-to-headline-2027-super-bowl-half-time-show\">named Jay-Z</a> as the headliner for this season’s Super Bowl half-time show. His company, Roc Nation, produces the show under a 2019 deal <a href=\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-10-15/nfl-jay-z-s-roc-nation-extend-deal-on-super-bowl-halftime-show\">reportedly</a> worth $5m a year, and the agreement was extended for another five years in 2024. NFL commissioner Roger Goodell said his one request when the partnership began was that Jay-Z “<a href=\"https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/jay-z-headline-2027-super-bowl-halftime-show-1235648064/\">take the stage himself one day</a>“.</p><p>Goodell’s wish makes total sense: Jay-Z is the perfect choice for the half-time show, and that’s not a compliment. The NFL wants a shill who can still pass for a rebel, and nobody in American music plays that part better than Jay-Z.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/28/jay-z-halftime-show-super-bowl\">Continue reading...</a>\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
