    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvxd1phc-1etb",
      "title": "I struggled to come to terms with my mum’s death. Then I swam in the sea and suddenly felt connected to her",
      "description": "<p>My mother was a keen swimmer, and she lived with depression. I wonder if she found the same respite in the water that has helped heal me since she passed away</p><p>It was early morning in late September, off the coast of Kaş in Turkey. Watching the slightly choppy sea from a boat, the last thing I felt like doing was embarking on another 5km (3-mile) swim. Mind fog had sapped my energy, and my mood was decidedly Eeyore-like. But as I pulled on my cap and flippers, adjusted my goggles and took the plunge, something shifted.</p><p>Head in the water, the knotted feeling in my throat began to loosen. Breathing came properly. There was respite.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/27/i-struggled-to-come-to-terms-with-my-mums-death-then-i-swam-in-the-sea-and-suddenly-felt-connected-to-her\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/27/i-struggled-to-come-to-terms-with-my-mums-death-then-i-swam-in-the-sea-and-suddenly-felt-connected-to-her",
