    {
      "id": "art-guardian_world-mvpeg0pr-v42x",
      "title": "No 10 plays down UK joining EU single market after Burnham suggests referendum would be needed – UK politics live",
      "description": "<p>The prime minister previously said that he wanted a debate on how Britain can establish a closer relationship</p><p>Speaking at his rough sleeping summit in Downing Street this morning, <strong>Andy Burnham</strong> expanded on the claim he made on Good Morning Britain about tackling rough sleeping being cheaper than ignoring the problem. (See <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6adf32578f082a87cec57149#block-6adf32578f082a87cec57149\">11.13am</a>.)</p><p>He said:</p><p>We’ve also got to be conscious when we speak to the rest of the country, the taxpayers of this country, that actually to leave people sleeping rough has financial consequences. It doesn’t work for anybody and it doesn’t work for the public purse, because if you leave people in a position where they are in and out of A&amp;E, and in and out of a hospital bed, that doesn’t make sense for anybody.</p><p>There is a rock-solid business case to do something to end homelessness, to put people on a path to recovery, so that they can contribute once again. And that should be the thinking that we take to this conversation.</p><p>Burnham is throwing another £432m at a problem he failed to solve in Greater Manchester. Under Labour, rough sleeping has hit record highs – yet their answer is always more taxpayers’ money and more empty promises.</p><p>[John] Healey must use his budget this week to cut taxes for working people, get the ballooning benefits bill under control, and stop pushing up energy prices.</p><p>John Healey is expected to confirm on Wednesday that the full state pension will rise by £489 to £13,036 – equivalent to 3.9%.</p><p>However, the rise will push about one million people on low incomes who rely on the state pension above the £12,570 personal allowance threshold, meaning they would be dragged into paying income tax.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates",
      "publisherId": "guardian_world",
      "creatorId": "guardian_world",
      "creatorName": "The Guardian World",
      "creatorHandle": "@guardianworld",
      "creatorAvatar": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1585829365295-ab7cd400c167?w=120&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80",
      "platform": "news",
      "category": "Current Affairs",
      "currentAffairsCategory": "World News",
      "publishDate": "2026-10-26T15:03:25.000Z",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T15:03:25.000Z",
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-26T15:16:18.977Z",
      "lastCheckedAt": "2026-10-26T15:23:12.047Z",
      "contentHash": "9eaa374855fd4344",
      "isUpdate": false,
      "readingTimeMinutes": 5,
      "thumbnailUrl": "https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/9101b7d3ce50d4f8e23d844f2aae5d321100834c/380_25_885_708/master/885.jpg?width=1000&amp%3Bquality=85&amp%3Bauto=format&amp%3Bfit=max&amp%3Bs=73fce6395128ef28c5de6d9ad69e51d2&quality=85&auto=format&fit=max",
      "originalImageUrl": "https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/9101b7d3ce50d4f8e23d844f2aae5d321100834c/380_25_885_708/master/885.jpg?width=1000&amp%3Bquality=85&amp%3Bauto=format&amp%3Bfit=max&amp%3Bs=73fce6395128ef28c5de6d9ad69e51d2&quality=85&auto=format&fit=max",
      "imageSource": "publisher",
      "imageType": "photo",
      "imageCaption": "Official photograph from The Guardian World for \"No 10 plays down UK joining EU single market after Burnham suggests referendum would be needed – UK politics live\"",
      "imageAttribution": "Source: The Guardian World",
      "imageValidationStatus": "valid",
      "quickSummary": [
        "Official verified dispatch from The Guardian World: \"No 10 plays down UK joining EU single market after Burnham suggests referendum would be needed – UK politics live\".",
        "Key Context: <p>The prime minister previously said that he wanted a debate on how Britain can establish a closer relationship</p><p>Speaking at his rough sleeping summit in Downing Street this morning, <strong>And.",
        "Categorized under Current Affairs for global monitoring and reference.",
        "Original reporting published by The Guardian World. Direct canonical dispatch linked."
      ],
      "easySummary": "<p>The prime minister previously said that he wanted a debate on how Britain can establish a closer relationship</p><p>Speaking at his rough sleeping summit in Downing Street this morning, <strong>Andy Burnham</strong> expanded on the claim he made on Good Morning Britain about tackling rough sleeping being cheaper than ignoring the problem. (See <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6adf32578f082a87cec57149#block-6adf32578f082a87cec57149\">11.13am</a>.)</p><p>He said:</p><p>We’ve also got to be conscious when we speak to the rest of the country, the taxpayers of this country, that actually to leave people sleeping rough has financial consequences. It doesn’t work for anybody and it doesn’t work for the public purse, because if you leave people in a position where they are in and out of A&amp;E, and in and out of a hospital bed, that doesn’t make sense for anybody.</p><p>There is a rock-solid business case to do something to end homelessness, to put people on a path to recovery, so that they can contribute once again. And that should be the thinking that we take to this conversation.</p><p>Burnham is throwing another £432m at a problem he failed to solve in Greater Manchester. Under Labour, rough sleeping has hit record highs – yet their answer is always more taxpayers’ money and more empty promises.</p><p>[John] Healey must use his budget this week to cut taxes for working people, get the ballooning benefits bill under control, and stop pushing up energy prices.</p><p>John Healey is expected to confirm on Wednesday that the full state pension will rise by £489 to £13,036 – equivalent to 3.9%.</p><p>However, the rise will push about one million people on low incomes who rely on the state pension above the £12,570 personal allowance threshold, meaning they would be dragged into paying income tax.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian World, this report highlights developments in World News.\n\nHeadline: \"No 10 plays down UK joining EU single market after Burnham suggests referendum would be needed – UK politics live\"\n\n<p>The prime minister previously said that he wanted a debate on how Britain can establish a closer relationship</p><p>Speaking at his rough sleeping summit in Downing Street this morning, <strong>Andy Burnham</strong> expanded on the claim he made on Good Morning Britain about tackling rough sleeping being cheaper than ignoring the problem. (See <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6adf32578f082a87cec57149#block-6adf32578f082a87cec57149\">11.13am</a>.)</p><p>He said:</p><p>We’ve also got to be conscious when we speak to the rest of the country, the taxpayers of this country, that actually to leave people sleeping rough has financial consequences. It doesn’t work for anybody and it doesn’t work for the public purse, because if you leave people in a position where they are in and out of A&amp;E, and in and out of a hospital bed, that doesn’t make sense for anybody.</p><p>There is a rock-solid business case to do something to end homelessness, to put people on a path to recovery, so that they can contribute once again. And that should be the thinking that we take to this conversation.</p><p>Burnham is throwing another £432m at a problem he failed to solve in Greater Manchester. Under Labour, rough sleeping has hit record highs – yet their answer is always more taxpayers’ money and more empty promises.</p><p>[John] Healey must use his budget this week to cut taxes for working people, get the ballooning benefits bill under control, and stop pushing up energy prices.</p><p>John Healey is expected to confirm on Wednesday that the full state pension will rise by £489 to £13,036 – equivalent to 3.9%.</p><p>However, the rise will push about one million people on low incomes who rely on the state pension above the £12,570 personal allowance threshold, meaning they would be dragged into paying income tax.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/oct/26/andy-burnham-labour-brexit-eu-homelessness-immigration-budget-rosebank-reform-latest-news-updates\">Continue reading...</a>\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
      "keyFacts": {
