e need to make sure that every properly cast ballot is counted. If I&#8217;m someone who is concerned about election security and free and fair elections, what should I be looking out for? So, for example, I have heard that there is some anxiety about mail voting. If you want to cast a mail ballot, just know that you probably have a bunch of options in your state as to how you can cast it. You can certainly rely on the mail and have USPS deliver that ballot, but of course, we know occasionally mail gets delayed. Your state may have ballot drop boxes. You may have ballot drop-off at election officials&#8217; offices, or even at polling places. Find out your options. Yes, definitely find out your options. Voters should also check their voter registration status. Make sure it&#8217;s up to date with their current address. Of course, if you&#8217;re not registered to vote and you still have time in your state to register, please do register. Everyone should participate. Our democracy works best the more of us that can participate. If your registration date has not passed, don&#8217;t forget to register to vote. Vote with your friends, vote with your community, vote with your family. Voting should be joyful. It&#8217;s a civic experience that I think is really fun. I try to take my kids with me to vote because it&#8217;s exciting. They get stickers. Everyone loves stickers . It&#8217;s half the reason I go. It&#8217;s also a reminder. You get your sticker, other people see you, they know it&#8217;s Election Day, maybe they go out to vote. It&#8217;s a way to be part of your community and invest in your community and to choose who you want to be a leader of your community. What about after Election Day? Earlier this year, Democrats and Republicans in Congress circulated a letter asking members to commit to recognizing state-certified winners and &#8220;oppose any attempt to refuse or delay a seating of a member-elect.&#8221; We saw House Speaker Mike Johnson delay the swearing-in of an Arizona representative last year, That seems to me like maybe a preview of what might be to come. How fearful should we be of these post-election attempts to block members from being seated? I think those scenarios are highly unlikely. I understand why folks are worried about them, but the reality is, states have their own certification processes, which are laid out in state law and that are mandatory for them to follow. It is not discretionary whether states certify their elections. So once there is a new Congress, we have confidence that they will be seated according to how the voters actually cast their ballots. I want to end by thinking about Trump&#8217;s consistent questioning of the integrity of our election system. If he ultimately doesn’t try to actually steal the election, what is the long-term impact of his relentless focus on it and support for policies like the Save America Act? Just because we have won in many courts against this administration doesn&#8217;t delude me into thinking that this administration is just going to stop in this years-long campaign to sow doubt in our election systems. I think that is the intent. It&#8217;s to create a lack of confidence. It&#8217;s to discourage voters from participating. And to some degree, I think the administration has succeeded in creating some confusion about the rules or a lack of confidence. But the only way that that campaign to undermine our confidence and to sow distrust actually works is if voters stay home. The way to defeat that attempt to shake voters’ trust in democracy is to participate in our democracy and to turn out and to make their voices heard regardless of party, regardless of candidate. To say that we will not be made to fear participation in our democracy, but we are going to continue to seize it and take joy in it and show that we deserve a stronger representative democracy that serves all Americans. What are things that could happen to inspire trust in our electoral system? Are there reforms that you think might make the public feel a reinvigorated sense of confidence? There are certainly things that Congress could do to create a national floor of voter access and ensure that someone&#8217;s right to vote doesn&#8217;t depend on what state they live in. That includes basic reforms like same-day registration, online voter registration, universal access to mail voting that doesn&#8217;t require strict satisfaction of certain excuse requirements, which still exists in many states. I think it&#8217;s time for Congress to ban partisan gerrymandering. I also think it&#8217;s time to restore the Voting Rights Act, and Congress can enact a statutory right to vote that is meaningfully enforceable so that voters can go to court if their voting rights have been burdened, and they can get a real remedy. That has been really undermined by the Supreme Court, but Congress can and should respond. They have it within their power. The Constitution gives Congress the power to set rules for elections. They should.\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
