      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian World, this report highlights developments in World News.\n\nHeadline: \"Peta releases video showing experiments on infant monkeys at University of Washington\"\n\n<p>Animal-rights group released video footage that shows distressed baby macaques undergoing lab experiments</p><p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BX6A1hEqW0w\">Footage</a> released by the animal-rights group Peta reveals, for the first time, filmed experiments at the <a href=\"https://wanbrc.uw.edu/\">University of Washington’s National Primate Research Center</a> (WaNPRC), in which newborn monkeys were separated from their mothers and subjected to <a href=\"https://reporter.nih.gov/search/ecmx97Dcw0SIrFqWdODi1A/project-details/9985432#description\">neurological</a> tests after prenatal exposure to a neurotoxin.</p><p><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6511476/#S2\">Records</a> show that the infant monkeys, some just two days old, were used in research on domoic acid, a toxin produced by marine algae that accumulates in shellfish and can cause illness in humans. The experiments were part of a project that ran for six years until 2020.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/26/peta-monkey-experiment-video\">Continue reading...</a>\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
