    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0un-vkw5",
      "title": "Country diary: Jackdaws carve up the sky as dusk descends on the moors | Richard Smyth",
      "description": "<p><strong>Eldwick Crag, West Yorkshire:</strong> In the low light and the high wind, these corvids are preparing to roost for the night. And they seem to be enjoying themselves</p><p>Jackdaws like blustery weather the same way dolphins love choppy seas. There are faster flyers and more graceful flyers, but I can’t think of a bird that just enjoys flying as much as a jackdaw does. They bob and bank and yaw and wheel. And right now it’s roosting time in the copse above the quarry.</p><p>A jackdaw roost – or rather the prelude to the roost, the bedtime gathering, the swirl of small, clacking crows above and about the trees, like particles about to be drawn into a plughole – isn’t a murmuration. It’s neither as spectacular nor as enigmatic as the twilight displays of starlings; it’s a flock, plain and simple (plain as the jackdaw’s call of its own name, “jack”). But like everything jackdaws do, it’s full of bustling, garrulous life.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/26/country-diary-jackdaws-carve-up-the-sky-as-dusk-descends-on-the-moors\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/26/country-diary-jackdaws-carve-up-the-sky-as-dusk-descends-on-the-moors",
