    {
      "id": "art-guardian_world-mvpeg0pk-wqk1",
      "title": "Lula wins historic fourth term as Brazilian president in defeat of far-right Flávio Bolsonaro",
      "description": "<p>Leftist incumbent Lula says unprecedented comeback was a ‘defeat for denialism’ as Bolsonaro refuses to accept election result</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/oct/25/brazil-election-runoff-live-updates-flavio-bolsonaro-lula\">Lula wins election in stunning comeback – live updates</a></p></li></ul><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/luiz-inacio-lula-da-silva\">Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva</a>, the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/luiz-inacio-lula-da-silva\">Brazilian</a> president, has staged a dramatic <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/27/viscerally-dedicated-to-social-issues-behind-lulas-drive-to-win-final-term-in-brazils-crunch-election\">election fightback</a>, defeating his far-right opponent, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/fl-vio-bolsonaro\">Flávio Bolsonaro</a>, to claim a historic fourth term in power.</p><p>With 100% of votes counted the 80-year-old leftist received 4.4m votes more than Bolsonaro, with a 51.79% share to his 45-year-old rival’s 48.21%. However, soon after Brazil’s electoral authority confirmed Lula’s victory, Bolsonaro refused to concede defeat and asked for the results to be annulled.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/lula-wins-brazil-election\">Continue reading...</a>",
