    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvpccgs3-uddo",
      "title": "Mbappé double secures Real Madrid late victory at Barcelona after Raphinha leveller",
      "description": "<p>Thirteen years later, José Mourinho led Real Madrid into the Camp Nou as one team and them out again as another having secured the kind of late victory he loves and which opens up a whole new world. With just three minutes and the final reserves of energy remaining, a wonderful moment of skill from Jude Bellingham found Eduardo Camavinga and set them on one last run. Camavinga sprinted free, skilfully slipped Pau Cubarsí and pulled back for Kylian Mbappé to provide the finish, a little bit of history written.</p><p>“The world that loves football will have stopped to watch this and they won’t have gone disappointed; this was two teams that played to win,” Mourinho said. It had been an intense, breathless ride, a proper battle, and it was his team who won it. Arriving right on time, Mbappé scored his second to make it 2-1, the Camp Nou falling quiet and their team given no time to react. They had drawn level after Mbappé’s opener, Raphinha scoring, but could not do it again.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/25/barcelona-real-madrid-el-clasico-la-liga-match-report\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/25/barcelona-real-madrid-el-clasico-la-liga-match-report",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/25/barcelona-real-madrid-el-clasico-la-liga-match-report",
