      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian Environment, this report highlights developments in Science and Environment.\n\nHeadline: \"The Guardian view on Oxford Street’s pedestrianisation: cities should be designed around people, not cars | Editorial\"\n\n<p>The transformation of Europe’s busiest shopping street should be a case study in making cities greener, less congested and more inviting places to live</p><p>Sadiq Khan has been <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/davehillblog/2016/jun/19/pedestrianisation-of-oxford-street-pledges-trade-and-trade-offs\">trying to pedestrianise</a> Oxford Street since he first became the mayor of London in 2016. On Monday, after numerous setbacks, his plan will finally come to fruition. The busiest stretch of the nation’s famous shopping street will be closed to traffic in a scheme designed to improve the area and make it more attractive to pedestrians. It should be an emblematic case study in making cities greener, less congested, more inviting places to live and visit.</p><p>The Greater London Authority hopes it will rejuvenate a high street fallen on hard times. Since the pandemic, the number of vacant shops on Oxford Street has <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/news/2026/oct/22/everyone-hates-oxford-street-will-pedestrianisation-save-it\">more than quadrupled</a>. It estimates that the scheme will generate <a href=\"https://haveyoursay.tfl.gov.uk/38968/widgets/114814/documents/77220\">£82m of value a year in the area</a> by increasing footfall and encouraging visitors to linger. This impact remains to be seen, but <a href=\"https://urbanstudies.brussels/sites/default/files/2024-11/2024_TIBERGHIEN_Charlotte_OK.pdf\">research</a> from other cities suggests that shops experience higher sales once an area is pedestrianised. Given the prime minister’s focus on rejuvenating British high streets, whose <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/jan/29/doom-loop-of-decline-how-struggling-high-streets