    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0uo-9hnx",
      "title": "Koalas are cute, furry, iconic – and dying of chlamydia. But how well does the approved vaccine work?",
      "description": "<p>Queensland researchers say a published study contains ‘silly mistake’ that over-inflates vaccine’s efficacy but the regulator is standing by decision to approve it</p><p>At wildlife hospitals across eastern Australia, many koalas brought in have distinctive brown patches on their bottoms – a sign of urinary incontinence and a painful inflamed bladder. Others have red, weeping eyes, pointing to a bacterial disease that can cause blindness in the endangered marsupials.</p><p>Chlamydia infection, also known as chlamydiosis, is the most significant disease affecting the animals. The culprit is most commonly the bacterium <em>Chlamydia pecorum</em>, which is spread via mating or close contact.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/26/koalas-australia-chlamydia-vaccine\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/26/koalas-australia-chlamydia-vaccine",
