    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0un-6o94",
      "title": "U-turn on EV policy could stall UK’s take-up of EVs | Fiona Harvey and Jasper Jolly",
      "description": "<p>The 2030 target for 80% of new cars to have zero emissions could be cut to as low as half</p><p>Electric cars make people happy. More than nine in 10 EV drivers – 94% in a recent poll – are saving money, and a similar number would stick with electric for their next purchase, and recommend them to family and friends. Sales are soaring: more than half of the new cars sold in the UK last month were electric, if hybrids are taken into account, and about 28% of new car sales overall were electric only.</p><p>This is hardly surprising, given that soaring petrol and diesel costs have pushed up the price of a full tank to about £120. EV drivers are paying about 2.4p a mile.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/25/policy-u-turn-uk-electric-vehicles\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/25/policy-u-turn-uk-electric-vehicles",
