      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0un-wme7",
      "title": "Lights out: the island that goes dark to save young seabirds – in pictures",
      "description": "<p>Every autumn, Corvo Island in the Azores turns off its public lighting for a few key days to help fledgling Cory’s shearwaters as they attempt to fly to the ocean guided by the stars</p><p>Once a year, Corvo, the smallest and most northerly island of Portugal’s Azores archipelago, goes dark. Since 2019, the island’s 450 or so inhabitants have chosen to <a href=\"https://spea.pt/en/corvo-island-turns-out-the-lights-to-protect-seabirds/\">switch off the lights every autumn for five nights</a> to tackle the disastrous effects of artificial light on Cory’s shearwaters, a seabird endemic to the Atlantic Ocean.</p><p>It began in 1991 when the community noticed huge numbers of deaths among young shearwaters.</p><p>A newborn Cory’s shearwater chick</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/25/corvo-azores-corys-shearwaters-switch-off-saving-seabirds-aoe\">Continue reading...</a>",
