    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvpccgs8-yiay",
      "title": "Ian Holloway’s circus: why the manager’s antics are no laughing matter for fans",
      "description": "<p>Swindon manager has a history of viral outbursts but fans want a united club and manager they can support</p><p>In Ian Holloway’s often madcap managerial orbit, this was simply the latest in a steady succession of eccentric outbursts. Eyes bulging as he spoke, he boomed the letter “D” and promptly stabbed himself in the chest with an imaginary dagger. Next came the letter “M” alongside a blow from another invisible knife. Finally, a “W” followed by a jab to the heart. “Dead. Man. Walking,” he declared, pausing for effect between words and briefly falling into an Oscar-worthy zombie stupor. “That’s how it feels.”</p><p>To an offbeat press conference genre containing seagulls following trawlers and self-proclaimed special ones, it was a crazy new entry worthy of its subsequent viral nature. Holloway at his finest.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/25/ian-holloway-circus-swindon-outbursts-no-laughing-matter-fans\">Continue reading...</a>",
