    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvpccgs7-a04c",
      "title": "Glitch in Chelsea’s coding costs Alonso while resurgent Spurs are all systems go | Barney Ronay",
      "description": "<p>Home side broke the game open with some unique corner routines, but a goalkeeping error ruined the plan</p><p>There was an <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/24/chelsea-tottenham-premier-league-match-report\">odd interlude at Stamford Bridge</a> with 76 minutes gone and the score still 0-0, one of those moments where suddenly your brain seems to be on inside out, like trying to walk the wrong way down an escalator.</p><p>Tottenham had a corner on the left-hand side, in a game that had felt to that point like an extended practice session, a refinement of systems, the football equivalent of watching two middle-aged men methodically emptying the dishwasher, adding salt, descaling the hoses, tutting over knives incorrectly slotted, cups in the wrong row.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/24/chelsea-tottenham-premier-league-austin-macphee-mike-penders-roberto-de-zerbi\">Continue reading...</a>",
