    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvkhuamn-9mfo",
      "title": "Bournemouth enjoy night to remember as 10-man Milan fail to weather storm",
      "description": "<p>This was a momentous night in Bournemouth’s history, although the truth is they would have been given a far sterner examination by Coventry or Hull. Whatever this rotten impersonation of a Milan side was, it bore no relation to those that forged such a celebrated history. The visitors resembled holograms even before their goalkeeper Mike Maignan, with an extraordinary rush of blood resulting in a penalty and red card, rendered their task impossible. They never showed the slightest hint of putting up a fight.</p><p>Bournemouth simply swamped the seven-time European champions and, amid a swirling south coast storm that rarely let up, could depart singing in the rain. Amine Adli’s sumptuous late free-kick, curled left-footed from 22 yards, meant they could finish the evening with the flourish they deserved. Their joy was clear at full time and met disapproval from some of Milan’s staff, who appeared to take umbrage in a short-lived fracas near the tunnel afterwards.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/22/bournemouth-milan-europa-league-match-report\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/22/bournemouth-milan-europa-league-match-report",
