n they get billed for it. “The difference is before the Affordable Care Act, maybe they anticipated some kind of cost-sharing,” Hoagland said. “But after, they expect it to be free.” Sign up for the Good Medicine newsletter Our political wellness landscape has shifted: new leaders, shady science, contradictory advice, broken trust, and overwhelming systems. How is anyone supposed to make sense of it all? Vox’s senior correspondent Dylan Scott has been on the health beat for a long time, and every week, he’ll wade into sticky debates, answer fair questions, and contextualize what’s happening in American health care policy. Sign up here . It doesn’t have to be this way. Corlette’s team at Georgetown University recently released a proposal that Americans be entitled to three primary care and two urgent care visits every year with no cost-sharing. This approach has two virtues, she told me: It will help people feel like they are actually getting value from their insurance — the very complaint that Steve Kuo raised — and, because we know primary care helps prevent problems down the road, it should also improve people’s health in the long term. Take the pressure off that one free visit and let people see the doctor a few times a year without worrying about being charged. “In employer-based insurance, a lot of people feel like, ‘What the hell am I getting for my premium each month?’” Corlette said. “We wanted people to feel like they&#8217;re getting good value, and we wanted to get to better population health.” Hoagland also endorsed the idea of at least one free physician’s visit every year, no matter what’s discussed. In the long term, Jabbarpour said she’d like to see copays eliminated entirely for primary care. But any payment reforms are not going to happen immediately. What can we do right now? For one, doctor offices could make an effort to be as transparent as possible. Jabbarpour’s MedStar system has started calling people a couple of days before their appointment with a brief script that tells the patient that they could be charged if the conversation with their doctor touches on additional medical problems. If your doctor’s office doesn’t proactively bring it up, ask when you schedule your appointment or when they call you to confirm it. Worst-case scenario, ask your provider what they consider preventive care when your visit begins. You might also want to educate yourself on what services are generally considered preventive and should be available for free: Your insurer may make that information available on their website, or KFF has a database you can peruse . It’s not a perfect solution. “What ends up happening is patients come in, and they say, ‘I know I&#8217;m not supposed to talk to you about anything else, so I&#8217;m not going to tell you about anything else.’ And you don&#8217;t want that to happen,” Jabbarpour said. You should ask your doctor directly: What do you consider to be preventive care? Establish boundaries at the start. That way, you can at least be aware if you are going to get charged because the conversation crossed the line to discussing an active health problem. And if you want to see the underlying laws change, start organizing. Contact your state and federal reps and ask them what they are doing to ensure this stops happening. Things could be different: Jabbarpour said she hopes to one day work in a health system where the cost of a single primary care visit isn’t so high-stakes, where doctors and their patients can manage their health according to their particular needs, rather than worry about how to bill for one specific conversation that covers the whole range of somebody’s health — like primary care is supposed to do. “I think some patients can be managed by seeing us once every year, once every two years, and some patients need to see us monthly,” Jabbarpour told me. “To put all patients in the same box and say you can only see your primary care physician once a year if you want it paid 