I don&#8217;t have a ton of faith that my Republican colleagues are going to have any more strength. I want to look at these signature cases and not just focus on the president, but on the people who enabled him. I want young people coming out of school to say, “Should I go work for the Trump administration? That sounds fun. I might get an opportunity.” I want them to be like, “Lord, that might be buying myself a ton of problems that I don&#8217;t want, and so let me avoid participating in that.” I want that deterrence message sent. I have to ask about [2028]. A number of Democrats have said already that they&#8217;re thinking about running for president. Is that something you&#8217;re thinking about? I have been open that I&#8217;m looking at it. It does not need to be me. I just need someone who understands the mission. And if that person comes out of the big process, I will be the first gal to get on whatever team they&#8217;re on and help them win in the parts of the country that I know well. But I think what is clear to me is that the American public is looking for something different — that generational shift. And I think that there&#8217;s hopefully going to be lots of opportunities for lots of people who you might not be thinking of who haven&#8217;t been sitting waiting their turn who are going to get in the mix. And I think that&#8217;s a good thing. I wanted to ask about something on the 2028 front. One thing that we have heard consistently over the last year, and I think this is a consequence of Democratic trauma from 2024 and also 2016 — people&#8217;s fears about sexism when women run. I&#8217;ve heard so many people, including women voters, tell me they think it needs to be a white guy. I find it to be a kind of depressing takeaway of those elections. It&#8217;s wrong, and I know it&#8217;s wrong because this very question is what we&#8217;ve been digging into as I&#8217;ve traveled around the country. What I think is happening is right now, is the pu