      "detailedSummary": "Published by Vox News & Explains, this report highlights developments in National News.\n\nHeadline: \"This Democrat’s accountability plan has Trump fuming\"\n\nSen. Elissa Slotkin (D-MI) at a news conference in the US Capitol on June 18, 2026. | Tom Williams/CQ-Roll Call, Inc via Getty Images The 2026 midterms are less than two weeks away, which means we’ll soon have a better idea of what the next two years of American politics could look like. If Democrats win at least one chamber of Congress — and they’re comfortable favorites to control the House — they’ll enter 2027 with oversight power for the first time in Donald Trump’s second term. If they win the Senate, too, they’ll have even more power. Whatever the outcome, Michigan Sen. Elissa Slotkin wants her party to hit the ground running. In an interview with Vox’s Astead Herndon at the University of Chicago’s Institute of Politics , she explained that the key divide in the Democratic Party is no longer “moderate versus progressive.” “Particularly in the era of Trump, I think it is fight versus flight,” she said. “Regardless of your persuasion, do you believe that we need to think differently about how we exercise power and oversight in the era of Trump and if we don&#8217;t, we&#8217;re at risk of losing our country as we know it?” Slotkin, who says she’s on “Team Fight,” discusses what that Day 1 Democratic agenda should look like, how she plans to hold the Trump administration accountable, whether she’s considering a presidential bid, and more. Below is an excerpt of the conversation, edited for length and clarity. There’s much more in the full show, so listen to America, Actually wherever you get your podcasts or watch it on Vox’s YouTube channel . How does Congress reassert its role so that people believe in the kind of checks and balances that Trump has washed away? When I think about, hopefully, Democrats doing well in a few short weeks here, the election is the end of one phase, but a much more important phase then comes into focus, and that&#8217;s what you do with the power that you&#8217;ve won. If we manage to win the House and especially the House and the Senate, there&#8217;s three things that we need to do. One, we need to pick two or three big pieces of legislation that speak directly to people&#8217;s pocketbooks and go hard on those — I would say housing and healthcare. Number two, we do have to have accountability for the unbelievable corruption that we are living under right now. But you can&#8217;t just make that your whole personality. You have to pick signature cases, and you have to go after people and projects that are super egregious. Everyone knows the East Wing of the White House, and people feel that in a way that&#8217;s different from a nameless contract at the Pentagon or whatever. So you need to pick those signature cases and go hard on accountability, not just Trump. Because of course, there will be moments of, “Let&#8217;s go after Trump himself.” As someone who voted to impeach him twice, he&#8217;s still there, right? But the people around Donald Trump who are doing his bidding and enabling this have no such immunity. They have no immunity the way the president does. And to me, he couldn&#8217;t do any of this stuff without all of those people saluting and supporting it. So you go after the people who do his bidding. Third, we have to Trump-proof the government going forward so that no president of any party can ever abuse the system the way he did, because we figured out under Trump that a lot of the stuff that we thought were laws were actually just norms, standards, habits, expectations, good citizens. And shame was required. Shame was required. And when you have a president who has no shame, they just walk all over that. And then even for things that were laws, the penalties, the teeth are not there. So you make an ad that plays during a football game, a political ad paid for by US taxpayer dollars, your dollars. The penalty is like, you have to pay a fine. Well, if they have the money and they have all the keys to the government, they&#8217;re going to pay that fine and not worry about it. So how do we get teeth in the things that were laws and strengthen those other laws going forward? You mentioned Trump-proofing the government. At this point, he has stacked the courts with a lot of favorable folks. He controls the Republican Party, has installed people who do his bidding on each of those levels. Is that possible? It does seem a little hard to figure out how the government becomes Trump-proof when it&#8217;s beyond an individual at this point. One of the things I think is changing about politics is that there is a groundswell of energy around certain issues that is not partisan. The groundswell going on in the Midwest about data centers or Flock cameras or AI in general is not like, “I&#8217;m a Democrat, I&#8217;m a Republican.” It is organized and grassroots. Data centers went zero to 60 faster than any issue I&#8217;ve seen out there. And I think harnessing that outrage — and that outrage also, I think, is growing on the corruption side — so how do you harness that for an ethics package? And look, I don&#8217;t agree with Sen. Josh Hawley of Missouri on almost anything, but he has a bill to get rid of corporate PACs and money in politics. Working with strange bedfellows, I think there is still a lot we can do because of that upward pressure. The other piece I was going to ask about — you mentioned the impeachment efforts, and I feel like one of the things that came from those was a lack of belief from the public about the effectiveness of those efforts. Let&#8217;s say Democrats were to take back the House and possibly the Senate. Should impeachment be a focus of a potentially incoming Democratic House? I will say, because I still see him on the floor, that if Sen. Mitch McConnell had done the right thing, then Donald Trump would have been impeached after January 6, but he did not. And his legacy, along with his health issues, will be how he is remembered. I am into effectiveness, and if we have the House but not the Senate, I don&#8217;t have a ton of faith that my Republican colleagues are going to have any more strength. I want to look at these signature cases and not just focus on the president, but on the people who enabled him. I want young people coming out of school to say, “Should I go work for the Trump administration? That sounds fun. I might get an opportunity.” I want them to be like, “Lord, that might be buying myself a ton of problems that I don&#8217;t want, and so let me avoid participating in that.” I want that deterrence message sent. I have to ask about [2028]. A number of Democrats have said already that they&#8217;re thinking about running for president. Is that something you&#8217;re thinking about? I have been open that I&#8217;m looking at it. It does not need to be me. I just need someone who understands the mission. And if that person comes out of the big process, I will be the first gal to get on whatever team they&#8217;re on and help them win in the parts of the country that I know well. But I think what is clear to me is that the American public is looking for something different — that generational shift. And I think that there&#8217;s hopefully going to be lots of opportunities for lots of people who you might not be thinking of who haven&#8217;t been sitting waiting their turn who are going to get in the mix. And I think that&#8217;s a good thing. I wanted to ask about something on the 2028 front. One thing that we have heard consistently over the last year, and I think this is a consequence of Democratic trauma from 2024 and also 2016 — people&#8217;s fears about sexism when women run. I&#8217;ve heard so many people, including women voters, tell me they think it needs to be a white guy. I find it to be a kind of depressing takeaway of those elections. It&#8217;s wrong, and I know it&#8217;s wrong because this very question is what we&#8217;ve been digging into as I&#8217;ve traveled around the country. What I think is happening is right now, is the public is so in search of something different from the traditional. If you&#8217;re a good communicator, if you&#8217;re exciting, if you&#8217;ve got big ideas, you can surmount what are thought of as traditional deficiencies or traditional weaknesses. So if you&#8217;re a woman, if you’re African American, if you&#8217;re Muslim American, if you&#8217;re whatever, those things forever have been thought of as negatives. But if you&#8217;re the right person with the right energy, you can surmount that. I think we&#8217;re seeing that around the country already.\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
