      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxyd6-j7eb",
      "title": "Champions League review: PSG take a hit, Man City’s empty seats and teenage hope for Liverpool",
      "description": "<p>The reigning champions must deal with the absence of Khvicha Kvaratskhelia for some time, while fans at Anfield were thrilled by Rio Ngumoha</p><p>Paris Saint-Germain <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/20/paris-saint-germain-psg-barcelona-champions-league-match-report\">beat Barcelona 4-2 on Tuesday</a>, but the reigning champions lost the best player in last season’s tournament, Khvicha Kvaratskhelia. This autumn has seen much lobbying for the Ballon d’Or, with the Georgian a popular choice, despite his absence from the World Cup. He is expected to be out for six months, although Rodri’s comeback from serious injury – he was rested by Barcelona on Tuesday until coming off the bench in the 60th minute – shows it can take far longer than that for players to rediscover their highest level.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/22/champions-league-review-psg-take-a-hit-man-citys-empty-seats-and-teenage-hope-for-liverpool\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/22/champions-league-review-psg-take-a-hit-man-citys-empty-seats-and-teenage-hope-for-liverpool",
