    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxyd9-8mtf",
      "title": "Mark Allen reveals he made snooker return after 10-week stay in mental health clinic",
      "description": "<ul><li><p>Former world No 1 vows to show ‘fighting qualities’</p></li><li><p>‘I see the light now that I couldn’t see before’</p></li></ul><p>Mark Allen has revealed he spent 10 weeks in a mental health clinic before discharging himself to <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/20/mark-allen-snooker-northern-ireland-open-interview\">play in this week’s Northern Ireland Open</a>. In a long statement posted on X, the 40-year-old said he was “not OK” but vowed to show his renowned “fighting qualities” as he continues his recovery.</p><p>Allen’s 4-2 defeat by his friend and compatriot Jordan Brown on Tuesday was his first match since his world championship semi-final loss to Wu Yize in April, when <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/may/02/shaun-murphy-beats-john-higgins-world-snooker-championship-mark-allen-wu-yize\">he missed a black off its spot</a> that would have given him the win.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/21/snooker-mark-allen-northern-ireland-open-mental-health\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/21/snooker-mark-allen-northern-ireland-open-mental-health",
