    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxydc-xa85",
      "title": "Manchester City have been a joy to watch but they became detached from their roots | Philipp Lahm",
      "description": "<p>City behaved much like other top clubs but their rule-breaking deserves to be punished. The Bundesliga, where members have a controlling stake, can show the way forward</p><p>Do we have to reassess Manchester City now? In my columns, I have repeatedly engaged with this club and its key figures. Looking back at what I have written over the years, I believe it remains beyond question that the team have achieved greatness. I always loved watching them: they were a true unit on the pitch, a finely tuned orchestra.</p><p>I would also write all over again that the conductor, Pep Guardiola, masters his craft like few others. I could recognise his teams even if they were wearing the wrong shirts and the game was broadcast in black and white – by the passes, the distances between the players and the intelligence of their interplay. It is no wonder Guardiola inspires others to become managers; look at Vincent Kompany, Mikel Arteta, Enzo Maresca, and perhaps some time Ilkay Gündogan and Rodri.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/21/manchester-city-fifa-rules-grassroots-philipp-lahm\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/21/manchester-city-fifa-rules-grassroots-philipp-lahm",
