    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxyda-u7nu",
      "title": "Gabby Thomas, Melissa Jefferson-Wooden and the doping tensions dividing sprinting",
      "description": "<p>As women’s sprinting enters a golden era in the run-up to LA 2028, controversy surrounding a top coach threatens to overshadow the sport’s biggest stars</p><p>When Olympic 200m champion Gabby Thomas came second at the recent track and field <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/sep/10/glowing-javelins-10m-prize-fund-brave-new-world-athetics-ultimate-championship\">World Athletics Ultimate Championship</a> in Budapest, she tried her best to be diplomatic: “I am an equal-opportunity doping hater,” she said. “If you are actively doping in sport, I do take issue with that. I’m not saying any names, no names are mentioned. But if you look at yourself and you think that’s what you’re doing, then so be it.”</p><p>Thomas then went out of her way to congratulate her compatriot Melissa Jefferson-Wooden, who finished ahead of her that day, aware of the territory into which she was straying.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/21/gabby-thomas-melissa-jefferson-wooden-dennis-mitchell-doping-sprinting\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/21/gabby-thomas-melissa-jefferson-wooden-dennis-mitchell-doping-sprinting",
