      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxydd-4i4x",
      "title": "Troy Aikman and Joe Buck: what next for the NFL’s $165m TV power couple as they head for a split?",
      "description": "<p>The duo have carved out lucrative and successful careers as TV audiences elsewhere are falling. It seems they won’t be together for much longer</p><p>Troy Aikman knew when it was time to go. His <a href=\"https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/aikman-rides-into-retirement-1.267356\">aching body</a> made the decision for him when he ended his NFL career in 2001. A quarter of a century later, with one of the top quarterbacks of the 1990s established as half of the pre-eminent broadcasting duo in American sports, his potential exit from the booth is not so clearcut.</p><p>Aikman clearly feels his voice remains in fine working order, but his bosses at ESPN may not agree. The color commentator’s Monday Night Football play-by-play partner, Joe Buck, is reported to have <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7600181/2026/09/16/joe-buck-espn-nfl-monday-night-football/\">agreed a new deal</a> with the network worth $108m over six seasons (his previous deal was for five years at $15m a season), yet Aikman has claimed ESPN does not want to renew his contract.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/21/troy-aikman-joe-buck-nfl-espn\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/21/troy-aikman-joe-buck-nfl-espn",
