      "detailedSummary": "Published by The Guardian Sport, this report highlights developments in Sports.\n\nHeadline: \"Does anyone feature on more than two clubs' all-time top-10 scoring charts? | The Knowledge\"\n\n<p>Plus: the longest kit supply partnerships, more own goals (part II) and consecutive goalless draws</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"mailto:knowledge@theguardian.com\">Mail us</a> with all of your questions and answers</p></li></ul><p><strong>“Harry Kane is now Bayern’s seventh-highest scorer of all time, as well as being Tottenham’s highest scorer. The man he overtook at the top of that list, Jimmy Greaves, is also Chelsea’s seventh-highest scorer of all time. Do any other players feature on two (or more) clubs’ all-time top-</strong><strong>10 scoring charts?”</strong> asks Owen Powell.</p><p>A trawl through <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/series/theknowledge\">the Knowledge archive</a> reminded us that we have previously looked at players who are the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2021/sep/15/the-knowledge-what-is-the-longest-spell-a-footballer-has-sole-possession-before-scoring\">record goalscorer for more than one club</a>. This question is slightly different, as we’re focusing on goalscorers who are in the all-time top 10 for the most different clubs. There are dozens of players who appear for two clubs, including <strong>Sergio Ag</strong><strong>üero </strong>(Atlético Madrid and Manchester City), <strong>Kenny Dalglish</strong> (Celtic and Liverpool), <strong>Jimmy Greaves </strong>(Chelsea and Tottenham)<strong>, Lionel Messi </strong>(Barcelona and Inter Miami), <strong>Cristiano Ronaldo</strong> (Real Madrid and Al-Nassr) and <strong>Alan Shearer</strong> (Blackburn and Newcastle).</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/21/anyone-more-than-two-clubs-all-time-top-10-goal-scoring-charts-the-knowledge\">Continue reading...</a>\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the