    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxydd-hfpp",
      "title": "Too much Overlap? Gary Neville’s podcast empire knows no bounds",
      "description": "<p>The Overlap has gone daily, with Steven Gerrard and Joe Hart among the latest additions to an ever-growing family</p><p>Viewed in 2026, Nathan Barley, Chris Morris’s 2005 sitcom, shows <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2015/feb/10/nathan-barley-charlie-brooker-east-london-comedy\">remarkable prescience</a> about the last two decades of metropolitan life, from smartphone addiction and digital derangement to sheer existential vapidity. When Barley’s character referred to his occupation as “self-facilitating media node”, Morris and co-writer Charlie Brooker were way ahead on the creator economy. What the pair failed to predict was ex-footballers joining the sector in droves. These days, if you are a former footballer without a podcast or YouTube channel, then did you ever really play the game?</p><p>Setting aside the Gary Lineker Goalhanger empire, manspreading its centrist-dad agenda into issues way beyond guffaws with Micah Richards and Alan Shearer, there is Gary Neville to rule them all. Manchesterism’s second-most influential practitioner has been on heavy manoeuvres. Unlike Lineker’s productions, the Overlap’s output continues to stick largely to football; a rugby podcast was mothballed in February, though its cricket equivalent continues.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/21/the-overlap-gary-neville-podcast-empire-knows-no-bounds\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/21/the-overlap-gary-neville-podcast-empire-knows-no-bounds",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/21/the-overlap-gary-neville-podcast-empire-knows-no-bounds",
      "publisherId": "the_guardian_sport",
      "creatorId": "the_guardian_sport",
      "creatorName": "The Guardian Sport",
      "creatorHandle": "@guardiansport",
