    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0ur-3j7z",
      "title": "This Norwegian village is one of the fastest-warming places on Earth. How can its people save it?",
      "description": "<p>As glaciers melt and permafrost thaws, Longyearbyen, on the remote Arctic islands of Svalbard, faces climate-induced problems from tap water to housebuilding to landslides</p><p>• <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/tap-water-no-longer-drinkable-norwegian-town-climate-related-thawing-heavy-metals-arctic-longyearbyen-svalbard\">Heavy metals make Norwegian village’s tap water toxic</a></p><p>On a rugged mountain plateau of snow-dusted rocks, in the remote archipelago of Svalbard, a glaciologist, Andy Hodson, points out the “ears” of ice, white against the rock, that gave Foxfonna glacier its name, after the arctic fox.</p><p>The small glacier, on a mountain 8 miles (15km) outside the Norwegian village of Longyearbyen, is a popular summer hike for tourists who come here seeking the Arctic experience.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/21/norway-svalbard-environment-longyearbyen-climate-heating-glacier-permafrost\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/21/norway-svalbard-environment-longyearbyen-climate-heating-glacier-permafrost",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/21/norway-svalbard-environment-longyearbyen-climate-heating-glacier-permafrost",
      "publisherId": "guardian_environment",
      "creatorId": "guardian_environment",
      "creatorName": "The Guardian Environment",
      "creatorHandle": "@guardianeco",
      "creatorAvatar": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1470071459604-3b5ec3a7fe05?w=120&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80",
      "platform": "news",
      "category": "Current Affairs",
      "currentAffairsCategory": "Science and Environment",
      "publishDate": "2026-10-21T06:00:04.000Z",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-21T06:00:04.000Z",
      "firstSeenAt": "2026-10-26T15:16:19.156Z",
