      "imageSource": "fallback",
      "imageType": "fallback",
      "imageCaption": "The World Ledger Editorial Dispatch: Scientists finally uncover why statins can cause muscle pain",
      "imageAttribution": "ScienceDaily • The World Ledger Visual System",
      "imageValidationStatus": "fallback",
      "quickSummary": [
        "Official verified dispatch from ScienceDaily: \"Scientists finally uncover why statins can cause muscle pain\".",
        "Key Context: Scientists have uncovered a surprising reason why statins, the widely prescribed cholesterol-lowering drugs that help prevent heart attacks and strokes, can sometimes cause muscle pain, weakness, and .",
        "Categorized under Health for global monitoring and reference.",
        "Original reporting published by ScienceDaily. Direct canonical dispatch linked."
      ],
      "easySummary": "Scientists have uncovered a surprising reason why statins, the widely prescribed cholesterol-lowering drugs that help prevent heart attacks and strokes, can sometimes cause muscle pain, weakness, and even severe muscle damage. Using advanced imaging technology, researchers discovered that statins can attach to a crucial protein in muscle cells and force open a channel that normally controls calcium flow. This causes calcium to leak continuously, potentially damaging muscle tissue.",
      "detailedSummary": "Published by ScienceDaily, this report highlights developments in Science & Technology.\n\nHeadline: \"Scientists finally uncover why statins can cause muscle pain\"\n\nScientists have uncovered a surprising reason why statins, the widely prescribed cholesterol-lowering drugs that help prevent heart attacks and strokes, can sometimes cause muscle pain, weakness, and even severe muscle damage. Using advanced imaging technology, researchers discovered that statins can attach to a crucial protein in muscle cells and force open a channel that normally controls calcium flow. This causes calcium to leak continuously, potentially damaging muscle tissue.\n\nThe World Ledger maintains strict source attribution and preserves the publisher's original journalism without automated hallucination.",
