    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0ur-041r",
      "title": "Tap water no longer drinkable in Norwegian village as climate-related thawing releases heavy metals",
      "description": "<p>Warning for other Arctic communities as residents told not to use water in Longyearbyen in Svalbard, one of the world’s fastest-warming places</p><p>Residents of a remote village in Norway that is in one of the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/jun/15/new-data-reveals-extraordinary-global-heating-in-the-arctic\">fastest-warming places in the world</a> have been told they must no longer drink tap water due to heavy metal contamination that scientists have linked to the thawing permafrost.</p><p>Longyearbyen, <a href=\"https://www.lokalstyre.no/engelsk/longyearbyen-local-council/info-box-frontpage/2026-05-13-important-information-about-drinking-water---recommendation-not-to-drink-tap-water\">in the archipelago of Svalbard</a>, gets its main drinking water supply from a 1.5 mile-long (2.5km) dammed lake called Isdammen, or “ice pond”. Higher temperatures as a result of the climate crisis have thawed permafrost, leading to newly exposed bedrock leaching metals into the pond.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/tap-water-no-longer-drinkable-norwegian-town-climate-related-thawing-heavy-metals-arctic-longyearbyen-svalbard\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/tap-water-no-longer-drinkable-norwegian-town-climate-related-thawing-heavy-metals-arctic-longyearbyen-svalbard",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/tap-water-no-longer-drinkable-norwegian-town-climate-related-thawing-heavy-metals-arctic-longyearbyen-svalbard",
      "publisherId": "guardian_environment",
      "creatorId": "guardian_environment",
      "creatorName": "The Guardian Environment",
      "creatorHandle": "@guardianeco",
