    {
      "id": "art-guardian_environment-mvpeg0uq-12ox",
      "title": "Cop17 at a glance: everything you need to know about the UN’s nature summit",
      "description": "<p>This month’s biodiversity summit in Yerevan, Armenia, aims to push governments to meet the 23 targets agreed at Cop15 in 2022</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/can-countries-deliver-on-a-deal-to-save-the-worlds-plants-and-animals\"><strong>Can countries deliver on a deal to save the world’s plants and animals?</strong></a></p></li></ul><p>This month, nearly every country on Earth will return to the question of how to stem the rapid destruction of the natural world. Humans are driving it, but so far we have not found a way to stop it.</p><p>In late 2022, governments reached an ambitious <a href=\"https://www.cbd.int/doc/c/e6d3/cd1d/daf663719a03902a9b116c34/cop-15-l-25-en.pdf\">once-in-a-decade deal</a> to better protect nature amid scientific warnings that the planet is going through the largest loss of life since the extinction of the dinosaurs. Now, they are assessing their progress on the targets for the first time.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/environment-cop17-un-biodiversity-summit-yerevan-explainer-conservation\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/environment-cop17-un-biodiversity-summit-yerevan-explainer-conservation",
