    {
      "id": "art-the_guardian_sport-mvjlxydc-jlor",
      "title": "From Dallaglio’s medals to Cantona’s shorts, sporting memorabilia is big business | Emma John",
      "description": "<p>Like transfer fees and ticket prices, mementoes are one more marker of sport’s rampant self-inflation</p><p>The auctioneer cradled his gavel in his palm like a pipe, the handle pointing towards him. It gave his patter the air of a Victorian gentleman holding forth by the mantelpiece in a period drama. “Are you bidding, Wilf? You’re waving at me.” Under the hammer was a Barclays Manager of the Month trophy, complete with travel case, but no indication of which Premier League luminary once owned it. It outstripped its estimate and sold for £700.</p><p>An hour and a half into what Budds auction house dubbed its “Octoberfest” of sporting memorabilia, David Convery at the podium showed no sign of fatigue, save the occasional swig from a water bottle. There were 332 lots in total; Convery regularly manages sales with twice as many. In his three decades as an auctioneer he’s even secured some world-record prices, from the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2005/may/19/newsstory.sport12\">oldest surviving FA Cup</a> to Pelé’s 1970 World Cup final shirt.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/16/from-dallaglios-medals-to-cantonas-shorts-sporting-memorabilia-is-big-business\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "canonicalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/16/from-dallaglios-medals-to-cantonas-shorts-sporting-memorabilia-is-big-business",
